Package: home-library
Architecture: any
Depends: libvips42t64 (>= 8.18.7),
         nodejs (>= 24),
         tzdata,
         ${misc:Depends},
         ${shlibs:Depends},
Recommends: nginx | apache2,
Description: catalogue the books in a private library, from any browser
 A self-hosted web app for one or more private libraries: scan ISBN barcodes
 with a phone's camera, fill in titles, authors and covers from Open Library
 and Google Books, and track which shelf each book is on and how much room
 every shelf has left. Data lives in SQLite.
 .
 The package runs the app as a systemd service behind nginx or Apache, which
 reach it over a Unix socket under /library/.

Package: home-library-failover
Architecture: all
Depends: curl,
         home-library (>= ${source:Version}),
         iproute2,
         openssh-client,
         rsync,
         sqlite3,
         util-linux,
         ${misc:Depends},
Recommends: openssh-server,
            tailscale,
Description: hand Home Library between two nodes, one writable at a time
 Moves the live SQLite database and its cover images between two machines
 running home-library, so that exactly one of them serves the library and
 accepts changes. Ownership is recorded with a generation counter, so a
 stale copy can never overwrite a newer one, and a node whose peer cannot be
 reached refuses to promote itself unless told to.
 .
 The active node advertises a floating address over Tailscale and copies its
 database to the standby every hour. Nothing changes until
 `home-library-failover enable` is run; see README.Debian.
